messages:
Caroline Park: escalation — customer reports duplicate POSTs and double billing after our SDK retried a request.
Caroline Park: details: TypeScript Node app on Vercel (Node 20), using @redwood-inference/sdk v1.3.2 which uses undici.fetch under the hood. Customer saw 502, we retried and they got charged twice.
Ben Liu: ack. Can you paste the minimal repro and any request/response logs (headers + status) you have? :eyes:
Caroline Park: snippet they provided:
```ts
const ac = new AbortController();
const res = await fetch(url, {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {'content-type': 'application/json'},
  body: streamBody, // ReadableStream from req
  signal: ac.signal
});
// on error they retry after backoff USING SAME ac
```
Caroline Park: they do exponential backoff and call fetch again reusing the same AbortController and the same ReadableStream body
Raj Patel: immediate red flag: reusing a ReadableStream or the same AbortController across retries is risky. undici/global fetch won't rewind a streamed body. That can lead to the upstream receiving partial or duplicate chunks depending on timing.
Raj Patel: also, some servers will accept repeated requests if the connection was reset mid-request — without idempotency keys you can get duplicate side effects (billing).
Emily W.: customer supplied server logs: two distinct POSTs received ~200ms apart, both processed. First return 502 to client, but server had already processed it.
Ben Liu: do we know undici version? Any SDK layering that clones body automatically?
Caroline Park: customer reported undici 5.16.0 on Node 20. SDK v1.3.2 doesn't clone streams; it forwards whatever body was passed to fetch.
Raj Patel: repro I ran locally (small script) — when body is a string it works fine. When it's a ReadableStream you need to recreate the stream per attempt or buffer the body. Example workaround:
```ts
async function sendWithRetries(body) {
  for (let i=0;i<3;i++){
    const bodyCopy = typeof body === 'string' ? body : bufferFromStream(body);
    const ac = new AbortController();
    try {
      await fetch(url, {method:'POST', headers:{'idempotency-key': idemp}, body: bodyCopy, signal: ac.signal});
      return;
    } catch(e) {
      await wait(backoff(i));
    }
  }
}
```
Raj Patel: bufferFromStream above is essentially Materialize stream into Buffer so fetch can reuse it safely.
Ben Liu: short-term mitigation: recommend customer add an Idempotency-Key header for non-idempotent ops. Also use stringified JSON bodies or buffer the stream before retries.
Emily W.: billing flagged this as charge duplication. Billing team wants rollback/credit for the duplicate charge. billing-bot: tagging billing.ops — please review transaction ids and refund the duplicate charge. :money_with_wings:
Caroline Park: customer is asking if this is an SDK bug — they expected SDK retry logic to be safe by default.
Raj Patel: SDK shouldn't silently retry non-idempotent requests with non-rewindable bodies. Two options:
- SDK detects non-idempotent method (POST/PUT/PATCH) with non-string/body stream and refuses to auto-retry unless idempotency-key provided OR
- SDK buffers small bodies internally (configurable size limit) and recreates body per attempt.
Ben Liu: agree. Could also add a warning in logs + telemetry when a retry happens on a non-rewindable body.
deploy-bot: created issue #INF-892: "prevent unsafe retries for non-rewindable bodies" and assigned to eng-runtime team.
Caroline Park: customer approved temporary workaround; wants ETA for SDK patch.
Raj Patel: I'll own a small fix: (1) detect stream/non-buffered body + non-idempotent method -> return error instead of retrying; (2) document best practices. Plan: PR tomorrow, patch release v1.3.3 within 48h.
Ben Liu: please include a changelog entry and a migration note for serverless users (Vercel/Cloud Run) who often use streams.
Emily W.: billing team confirmed initial refund for the duplicate charge, following up on the rest.
Caroline Park: thanks everyone. I'll follow up with the customer and share workaround steps.
Ben Liu: tl;dr for support playbook: if a customer sees duplicate side effects after SDK retry, check: method, body type (stream vs string/buffer), idempotency-key presence, undici version, and request logs. Recommend idempotency keys or buffering for POSTs.
Caroline Park: closing loop — I'll paste the patched repro script into the support thread and update the KB.
